00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.880
The first question is
about how to improve sleep.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.460
In particular, how to improve deep sleep

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.680
that is sometimes called slow-wave sleep.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.750
This was a question that
was asked by Jack Pritchard

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.180
and they got a lot of up votes.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.880
Now, we've done multiple
full-length episodes

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.770
of the Huberman Lab podcast on sleep

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.300
and tools to improve sleep,

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.150
including the Master Your Sleep episode

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.220
and Perfect Your Sleep episode,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.350
as well as a toolkit that is available

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.510
completely zero cost at hubermanlab.com.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.820
It's a downloadable PDF that lists out

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.260
the behavioral tools and other tools

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.780
that you can implement
to improve your sleep.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.760
Now Jack's question is specifically about

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.680
how to improve deep sleep.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.980
He asks, I have a sleep
analyzer and I typically observe

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.870
that I get a lot of REM sleep,

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:11.850
that is rapid eye movement sleep.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.340
For him, he says it's
30% of his total sleep

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.650
is rapid eye movement sleep.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:21.650
But he asks, my deep sleep
is often low, around 10%,

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:22.860
and I would like to know if there

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.330
are any science-backed protocols

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.660
I can use to specifically
increase my deep sleep.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.280
Okay, so for those of
you that may not know

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.900
what is deep sleep.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.570
Now, deep sleep is sometimes
referred to as slow-wave sleep,

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.780
that is slow-wave sleep and
deep sleep are sometimes used

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.210
interchangeably to
describe the same thing.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:40.950
They aren't necessarily the same thing,

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:44.460
but they fall under the
same mechanistic umbrella.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.290
What is that mechanistic umbrella?

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.900
Well, when you go to sleep at night,

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.590
you cycle through a number
of different stages of sleep.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.540
And in the early part of the night,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.900
I would say in the first
half of your night or so,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.770
most of your sleep is going
to be slow-wave deep sleep.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.110
But then as you transition
into the second half

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:05.580
of your night, more of your sleep

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.830
will be rapid eye movement sleep.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.660
Now that's the typical pattern

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.820
that is observed when
you're not disrupting

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.440
your sleep architecture with things

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:14.670
like caffeine and alcohol.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.250
We'll talk about caffeine
and alcohol in a few minutes.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.380
Deep sleep, aka slow-wave sleep,

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.990
at least for sake of this conversation,

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.570
is vitally important in particular

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.910
for repair of bodily tissues.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.700
During deep sleep, you
secrete growth hormone

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.250
and in fact you secrete
growth hormone every night

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.420
when you go to sleep.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.190
But most of that growth hormone release

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:39.450
is restricted to the first
half of your sleep night.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:40.710
The other feature of deep sleep

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.590
is that the dreams that
occur during deep sleep

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.530
tend to be of a less emotional nature

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:48.630
than the sorts of dreams
that one experiences

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.550
in rapid eye movement sleep.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.910
So just to give a broad top
contour of what deep sleep,

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.490
aka slow-wave sleep is really about,

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.730
it tends to occur in the
first half of the night,

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.350
it also occurs in the
second half of the night,

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:02.430
but in the first half of the night,

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.580
most of your sleep is going
to be occupied by deep sleep,

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.110
aka slow-wave sleep.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.900
It is the time when your
bodily tissues are repaired

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.790
in part by release of growth hormone,

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.080
which is involved in metabolism,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.083
but also protein synthesis, muscle repair,

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.620
repair of all cell types and tissues.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.120
And during deep sleep,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.890
your dreams tend to be somewhat mundane,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:24.870
sometimes a little bizarre,

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.240
but typically not very emotionally loaded.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.410
That can change if you're dealing

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:31.560
with a lot of emotionally
laden circumstances

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.420
in your waking life, but in general,

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.520
the sleep and dreams that
occur in the first part

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.530
of the night is deep

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.800
and it's not terribly emotionally ridden.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.463
Your body also goes through
specific patterns of metabolism.

00:04:44.463 --> 00:04:48.090
Now, this relates to recent
findings just this last year

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:49.838
in which researchers monitored

00:04:49.838 --> 00:04:52.470
the output of people's breath

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:55.860
and the various metabolic
factors present in the breath

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.600
as well as through blood draws,

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.740
So basically doing all this

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:00.477
while people are asleep in a laboratory.

00:05:00.477 --> 00:05:02.670
And they observe that during
the first half of the night

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:04.590
while people are in deep sleep,

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:07.980
their body is actually metabolizing
energy very differently

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.710
than it does in the
second half of the night.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.570
I'll come back to this in a little while,

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:13.950
but this turns out to be very important

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.170
for establishing your metabolism

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:18.780
and the way you regulate
insulin and blood glucose

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.100
during the daytime.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.640
So all of this is to say that
getting sufficient deep sleep,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.100
slow-wave sleep, is vitally important.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.350
And Jack's asking how he
can increase the amount

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.090
of deep sleep that he's getting because

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:32.070
by his sleep tracking method,

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:34.770
he's observing far less deep sleep

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.843
than rapid eye movement sleep.

00:05:37.793 --> 00:05:39.480
Now that raises the question
of how much deep sleep

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.520
one should be getting overall.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.220
In general, the goal should be to balance

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:45.930
the total amount of slow-wave sleep

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:48.300
and rapid eye movement
sleep across the night.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.120
However, I would not want people to obsess

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:54.240
over getting exactly 30%
rapid eye movement sleep

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.400
and exactly 30% slow-wave sleep.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.200
Your body is much more
resilient than that,

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.840
but the closer those two
numbers are to one another,

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.330
the better overall
architecture of your sleep.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.100
And he doesn't list out exactly

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:06.870
how he's measuring his sleep

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:10.200
so I do want to put an
asterisk on both the question

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.090
and the answer I provide by stating

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:13.470
that a lot of sleep trackers,

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:16.020
whether or not they are
WHOOP trackers or Ouras

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:18.240
or whether or not you're
using a mattress based

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.890
or mattress cover based sleep tracker

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.750
like Eight Sleep or other,

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.510
is using heart rate and
heart rate variability

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:28.260
and body movement to estimate

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:31.440
or to kind of predict whether or not

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.450
you're in slow-wave sleep
or rapid eye movement sleep.

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.040
And that's because during
rapid eye movement sleep,

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.200
you tend to be in what's called atonia,

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.810
you tend to be paralyzed, you can't move.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.720
And during slow-wave
sleep, that's not the case.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.840
But no matter what the
sleep tracking method is,

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.470
if it's a standard commercial
sleep tracking method,

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.630
WHOOP or Eight Sleep, et cetera,

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:54.450
it is an estimate or a best guess

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.760
at what stage of sleep you're in.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.680
Whereas when you go
into a sleep laboratory,

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.210
like the sleep laboratory at Stanford,

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.610
at University of Pennsylvania or other

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.590
of the great sleep laboratories
that are out there,

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.300
they're going to use other methods

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.210
including EEG and EMG.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:11.040
These are just acronyms that refer to ways

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.510
to record brainwaves.

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.520
They actually are going to try and observe

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:18.120
for rapid eye movements
beneath the eyelids, et cetera.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.940
So sleep trackers give you a best guess

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.800
as to what stage of sleep you're in.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:24.330
They are not perfectly accurately,

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:26.940
at least none of the
commercially available

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:28.920
sleep trackers are perfectly accurate.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.510
So we do want to highlight that.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.700
Okay, so now let's answer
Jack's question directly,

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.040
which is how to increase the total amount

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:35.873
of slow-wave sleep.

00:07:35.873 --> 00:07:37.440
Well, there are a couple ways.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:38.910
First of all are the don'ts

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.220
and then we'll talk about the dos.

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.960
Two things that you can
do to really disrupt

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.880
your slow-wave sleep

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:48.840
are to drink alcohol
within eight hours prior

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:50.280
to bedtime or even at all.

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:52.140
Some people will find
that even if they have

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:53.760
a glass of wine or a beer with lunch,

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.170
that the amount of slow-wave sleep

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.720
and sometimes even the
rapid eye movement sleep

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.090
that they get at night is reduced

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:03.300
and that their overall sleep
architecture is disrupted.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.160
This has been shown again and again.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.460
Likewise, avoiding caffeine within eight

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:11.550
and ideally within 12 hours of bedtime

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:13.680
would be preferable.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:14.940
And if you're not a caffeine drinker,

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:16.470
obviously you don't have
to worry about this at all,

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:19.440
but avoiding caffeine intake
within that 8 to 12 hours

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:22.170
of bedtime will greatly assist in you

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.210
getting more slow-wave sleep

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:26.460
and higher quality rapid
eye movement sleep.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:28.710
This has been shown again and again.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:32.670
And as well avoiding cannabis use

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:37.410
and maybe even CBD use
within 8 to 12 hours of sleep

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:39.960
can be very beneficial if your goal

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:41.730
is to improve the quality of your sleep.

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:44.640
Now, I know a lot of
people rely on alcohol,

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.240
cannabis and CBD in
order to get into sleep.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:51.510
However, it's been shown
many times in human studies,

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:53.910
and this was discussed
in the podcast episode

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:55.680
that we did with the great Matt Walker,

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.230
who's a professor of
neuroscience and psychology

00:08:58.230 --> 00:08:59.640
at University of California, Berkeley,

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.760
and a world expert in
sleep and sleep science.

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:04.350
Matt and I discussed the research showing

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.180
that even though alcohol
helps you fall asleep,

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:08.340
even though cannabis can
help you fall asleep,

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:10.830
and even though CBD can
help people fall asleep,

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:14.070
that it does disrupt
and in many ways reduce

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:16.470
the quality of sleep that one gets.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:18.090
So that's disappointing news I know

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:19.890
for a number of the people
that rely on cannabis,

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.660
CBD, or alcohol to fall asleep,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.150
but I would encourage you
to check out the episodes

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:25.650
of the Huberman Lab podcast that we did

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.800
about alcohol and about cannabis,

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.630
including some of the
health benefits of cannabis

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:32.040
in certain situations,

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:33.930
but also some of the detrimental effects

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:36.690
of both alcohol and
cannabis in other situations

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.790
in particular for
improving slow-wave sleep.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:41.010
So that's actually three major don'ts.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:44.400
Avoid caffeine, alcohol, and cannabis,

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.920
and let's put under the
umbrella of cannabis,

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:51.920
THC and CBD in the 8 and
ideally within the 12 hours

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.290
prior to sleep if your goal is to improve

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:58.350
the total amount of slow-wave
sleep that you're getting

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:00.990
and your overall sleep architecture.

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:01.863
In addition,

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.210
it's well known that avoiding food intake

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.370
in the two hours prior to sleep

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:11.760
is going to greatly assist in the amount

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:13.650
of growth hormone output that you achieve

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:15.210
during the early stages of the night

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:19.230
and the depth and duration
of slow-wave sleep.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:21.750
Now, I want to place an
asterisk on that statement

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:25.020
by saying if you go to sleep too hungry,

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:26.670
it can also disrupt your sleep.

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.010
So you're going to have to
arrange your eating schedule

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:30.840
and your sleeping schedule such that

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:32.850
you're not eating too close to bedtime,

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:35.670
but you're also not going to bed so hungry

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:37.470
that you can't fall asleep
or that you don't get

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.300
into deep sleep and stay asleep.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:40.920
I can personally attest to the fact

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:42.750
that if I'm too hungry,

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:43.740
I can't fall asleep.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:46.230
Now for me, most nights
I'm going to eat dinner

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:48.630
sometime between 6:00 PM and 8:00 PM

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:50.220
depending on time of year, schedules,

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:51.840
what's going on, traffic, et cetera.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.060
And I tend to go to sleep
somewhere between 10:00 PM

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.490
and 11:00 PM plus or minus an hour,

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:58.320
again, depending on time of year

00:10:58.320 --> 00:10:59.970
and what else is going on in life.

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:02.940
So I normally have that
buffer of two hours or so

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:04.050
before going to sleep.

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:05.280
However, there are some nights

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.410
where even if I eat dinner around 7:00 PM,

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:11.610
I'm quite hungry at 9:00 PM
and I want to go to sleep.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:13.923
In that case, eating a
small amount of food,

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:17.730
ought not to disrupt your
slow-wave sleep too much,

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:19.710
but you do want to
avoid eating large meals

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:22.260
right before going to sleep unless,

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:26.850
unless completely fasting
and not eating something

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:28.320
late at night will
prevent you from getting

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:29.490
into any sleep at all.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:30.810
In other words, it's better to make sure

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:32.460
that you're getting to sleep

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:35.460
than it is to avoid food
just so that you get

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:38.580
increased growth hormone output
and into slow-wave sleep.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:40.050
I hope that's clear.

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.270
You know, I have had times in my life

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:43.410
where I'm working very, very hard

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:46.110
and I missed dinner or something comes up

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:48.810
and I know some people can
fall asleep quite easily

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:50.010
on an empty stomach.

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:50.910
I've been able to do that,

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:52.950
in particular if I'm very exhausted.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:55.170
I also rely on a handful of supplements

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:57.600
to fall asleep each night,

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.460
although I've not dependent on them.

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:01.230
There have been times when I
haven't been able to access

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:03.180
those supplements and I
can still fall asleep.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:05.040
But the point is that you
don't want to be so hungry

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:06.870
that you can't fall asleep.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:09.150
And yet in an ideal circumstance,

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:10.590
especially if you're trying to increase

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:11.760
the amount of slow-wave sleep,

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:14.760
you would avoid food
in the two hours or so

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:16.230
before going to sleep.

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:18.510
If you can avoid food for
the three or four hours

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:21.450
prior to sleep and still
fall and stay asleep easily,

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:24.330
that's even better for sake
of increasing slow-wave sleep

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:26.100
and growth hormone output.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:28.200
Now, there are some additional tools

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:29.970
for improving slow-wave sleep,

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:33.270
in particular, the transitions
between slow-wave sleep

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:34.740
and the other sleep stages

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:36.840
because even though,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:39.240
the early part of your
night is occupied primarily

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:40.950
by slow-wave sleep, all night long

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.350
you're transitioning from slow-wave sleep

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:45.000
into an intermediate stage of sleep

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:46.440
and then into rapid eye movement sleep

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:47.273
and then back again.

00:12:47.273 --> 00:12:49.260
It's just that in the
early part of the night,

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:51.120
more of that time is going to be occupied

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:52.173
by slow-wave sleep.

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.020
The two ways to improve slow-wave sleep

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:56.850
that are well documented
in the literature,

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:58.740
and here we can point to
some really nice papers

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:01.500
that I'll reference in case
you want to read further,

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:03.540
is the first one is entitled,

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.820
Exercise Improves the
Quality of Slow-Wave Sleep

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:08.640
by Increasing Slow-Wave Stability.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:10.800
Slow-wave Stability has to do with,

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:13.500
as researchers call it,
the amount of power present

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.450
in the different aspects
of slow-wave sleep,

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:17.070
So this gets a little bit technical,

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:19.710
but this is an instance in
which they brought subjects

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:23.730
into the laboratory, they
measured brainwaves by EEG,

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:25.020
they had some other measurements as well

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:27.090
included in subjective measures of sleep,

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:28.800
and they looked at whether or not people

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:32.520
were doing exercise or not prior to sleep.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:33.960
Now, when I say prior to sleep,

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:36.810
I mean not within six
hours prior to bedtime.

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:41.070
In fact, exercising
intensely in the six hours

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:43.110
prior to bedtime, for
many people, not all,

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:45.690
but for many people can
disrupt the total amount

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:47.310
of slow-wave sleep that they get

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.290
and can also disrupt
rapid eye movement sleep.

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:52.170
So the conditions of the study
were pretty straightforward.

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:55.440
It was a randomized crossover trial.

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.690
Basically, they had people either sleeping

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:58.710
and monitoring their sleep

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:03.120
or they had people doing
an hour of exercise at 60%

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:06.270
of their so-called VO2
max, you can look this up,

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:07.740
for 60 minutes.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:11.100
60% of their VO2 max,
that was the intensity.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:13.590
It's breathing pretty hard,
but not extremely hard.

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:15.930
If we were going to map
that to the so-called zone,

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:17.670
zone 2 cardio being the kind of cardio

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:20.400
you can do steady state
while holding a conversation.

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.560
This would be somewhere
in the probably zone 3

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:26.520
or zone 4, although that's not exactly

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:30.360
what 60% of VO2 Max is
going to map to every time.

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:35.280
Think about relatively
challenging cardiovascular output

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:38.310
for 60 minutes, and they had them do that

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:40.860
at least six hours prior to bedtime,

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:42.690
and, this is an important and,

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:45.030
They did have the subjects in this study

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:48.540
in all conditions abstain
from caffeine and alcohol,

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:49.650
so that's very important.

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:52.080
And what they observed was an enhancement

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:53.520
of slow-wave sleep.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:55.920
So exercise of this sort turns out

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:58.890
to be a very potent form of
improving slow-wave sleep.

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:00.900
Now, I do not know, because the study

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:02.640
as far as I know has not been done,

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:05.100
whether or not doing resistance training

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:07.020
or some other type of exercise

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:08.730
would've led to the same effect,

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:11.550
although I have to imagine
that if it's moderately intense

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:13.590
to intense resistance training

00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:17.280
provided it's done far enough
away from going to sleep

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:19.710
prior to six hours before sleep,

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:22.260
that one ought to see the same effects.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:24.390
Although that was not a
condition in this study.

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:25.860
But it's a very nice study.

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:27.600
They looked at everything from changes

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:30.630
in core body temperature
to caloric expenditure.

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:33.390
They didn't see huge changes in core body

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:36.300
temperature changes, so that
couldn't explain the effect.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:38.970
It really appears that the major effect

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:41.880
of improving slow-wave sleep was due

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:44.880
to something in changing
the fine structure

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:47.790
of the brain waves that
occur during slow-wave sleep.

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:50.430
In fact, and this is an important point,

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:54.180
the subjects in this study
did not report subjectively

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:56.730
feeling that much better from their sleep.

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:59.160
So you might say, well then why
would I even want to bother?

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:02.190
However, it's well known
that getting sufficient

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:05.160
slow-wave sleep is important
not just for repair,

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:07.830
excuse me, for repair of bodily tissues,

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:10.200
but also for repair of brain tissues

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:13.920
and repair and wash out
of debris in the brain.

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:18.150
And that debris is known to
lead to things like dementia.

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:20.970
So getting the best
quality slow-wave sleep

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:24.270
is extremely important
for body and for brain.

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:25.950
And getting an hour of exercise

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:27.900
that's moderately intense and making sure

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:29.670
that you're not doing that exercise

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:32.010
within the six hours prior to bedtime,

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:35.070
and avoiding caffeine and alcohol,

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:37.020
certainly within the six
hours prior to bedtime,

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:39.930
but I would say in the 8 to 12 hours

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:41.610
prior to bedtime would be even better

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:43.920
or not drinking alcohol at all,

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:45.360
or I know there are people out there

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:46.560
that don't drink caffeine at all,

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:47.730
I'm not one of those people,

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:49.140
but restricting that caffeine intake to

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:51.000
the very early part of the day,

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:54.870
that has been shown to improve the quality

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:56.610
and the overall architecture of sleep

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:58.260
and slow-wave sleep in particular.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:16:59.850
That's really what this study points to.

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:02.370
There's another tool that
can improve the amount

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:05.524
and quality of slow-wave sleep
that you achieve at night.

00:17:05.524 --> 00:17:07.109
This is actually a tool
that I've started using

00:17:07.109 --> 00:17:09.333
over the last six to eight months or so.

00:17:10.350 --> 00:17:11.430
As many of you know,

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:14.400
I believe in getting behaviors right

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:17.490
before embracing changes
in supplementation

00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:19.140
or prescription drugs.

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:21.720
Behavioral tools consist
of dos and don'ts,

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:24.120
and the dos and don'ts for sleep

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:27.330
are well documented in
the Master Sleep episode

00:17:27.330 --> 00:17:28.560
and the Perfect Your Sleep episode

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:29.550
and the toolkit for sleep.

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:31.230
They include getting morning
sunlight in your eyes

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:32.280
or bright light of other kinds,

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:34.800
avoiding bright light
from 10:00 PM to 4:00 AM,

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:36.090
et cetera, et cetera.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:38.220
All that information is
in the toolkit for sleep

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:40.050
and those other full-length episodes

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:41.850
of the Huberman Lab podcast.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:45.750
Now, there are supplements
that can greatly improve

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:48.690
the depth of your sleep

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:52.050
and that can shorten the
latency to fall asleep.

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:53.700
And here I'm not referring to melatonin,

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:55.320
as many of you perhaps already know,

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:57.990
I'm not a fan of melatonin
for a variety of reasons,

00:17:57.990 --> 00:18:01.380
mainly the fact that most
melatonin supplements out there

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:04.020
don't actually contain the
exact amount of melatonin

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:04.980
that's listed on the bottle.

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:08.160
They contain either much,
much more or much, much less.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:10.650
And that melatonin is a potent hormone

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:13.290
that has impacts on not just sleep,

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:15.420
but on other hormone systems as well.

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:18.600
The supplements that I've suggested

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:22.020
and that I personally take
in order to improve my sleep

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:27.020
are magnesium threonate,
spelled T-H-R-E-O-N-A-T-E.

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:29.940
And interchangeably with that

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:32.940
one could use magnesium bisglycinate.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:34.290
I do consider those interchangeable

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:36.330
because both cross into cells

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:37.860
and cross the blood brain barrier

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:40.710
in much the same way that
makes them better candidates

00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:41.790
for improving sleep

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:43.920
than some of the other forms of magnesium.

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:48.900
So magnesium threonate,
theanine, T-H-E-A-N-I-N-E,

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:51.690
theanine, and something called apigenin,

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:54.990
which is essentially
what you find in camomile

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:58.950
that is the reason why
camomile can make you sleepy.

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:01.920
A-P-I-G-E-N-I-N, apigenin.

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:04.080
The dosages for those et cetera

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:06.300
are described in the toolkit for sleep

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:09.360
and why some people might
want to avoid theanine.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:10.530
I'll just tell you, for those of you

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:12.810
that have very intense
dreams or that might

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:14.580
sleepwalk or have night terrors,

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:15.810
you might want to avoid theanine

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:19.860
because it can enhance
the kind of emotional

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:22.080
and elaborate nature of dreams.

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:23.070
Other people like that.

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:26.100
So I'll take magnesium
threonate, theanine, and apigenin

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:28.860
as a so-called sleep stack
about 30 to 60 minutes

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:30.450
prior to sleep.

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:32.403
I do not take them during the day.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:35.280
There is, however, another supplement

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:37.830
that used in combination
with the sleep stack

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:41.040
that I just described or alone

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:44.880
can greatly enhance the
quality of your sleep.

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:47.400
And in particular,
people who are following

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:49.860
a low carbohydrate diet
who have trouble falling

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:52.260
and staying asleep seem
to benefit from it,

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:54.630
and that is so-called inositol.

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:56.400
Now, inositol comes in different forms,

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:58.560
but the form that I'm referring to here

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:02.220
in terms of enhancing slow-wave
sleep is myo-inositol.

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:05.310
There are an enormous number
of studies on inositol

00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:08.940
for sake of mental health and for sake of

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:11.130
enhancing various aspects
of cellular function

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:15.090
and for sake of improving sleep.

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:17.370
I'd like to just highlight one paper,

00:20:17.370 --> 00:20:19.500
title of this paper will make
it clear what it's about.

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:22.410
The title of the paper is
The Impact of Myo-Inositol

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:24.780
Supplementation on Sleep
Quality in Pregnant Women:

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:27.930
A Randomized Double-Blind
Placebo Controlled Study.

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:30.000
Now, as I just told you,

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.220
the study was carried
out on pregnant women,

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:34.740
but there have been
other studies of inositol

00:20:34.740 --> 00:20:36.420
on other populations.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:38.970
And the major takeaway from these studies

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:41.850
is that inositol can improve

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:45.600
the quality of sleep and
can adjust the architecture

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:49.320
of sleep in ways that
make it a great candidate

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:52.020
for improving the structure

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:53.820
and maybe also the duration

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:56.250
of slow-wave sleep in particular.

00:20:56.250 --> 00:21:00.030
I find that if I'm following
a lower carbohydrate diet,

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:01.950
which I do from time to time,

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:03.870
I sometimes have trouble falling asleep.

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:07.350
Or if I eat four to six
hours prior to bedtime,

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:09.360
then I tend to be pretty
hungry around bedtime.

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:11.760
And sometimes if I'm exercising very hard

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:12.690
or if I'm working a lot,

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:14.430
even if I eat dinner around seven

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:16.500
and I go to sleep around 10:00 PM,

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:19.830
I might be a little bit
hungry still at 10:00 PM.

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:22.440
In that case, I find that
taking 900 milligrams

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:26.010
of myo-inositol in addition
to the other sleep stack

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:29.010
that I just described
greatly enhances my ability

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:32.040
to fall asleep and to get
terrific quality sleep.

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:34.170
I do monitor my sleep.

00:21:34.170 --> 00:21:35.250
I do that two ways.

00:21:35.250 --> 00:21:39.660
I do that these days with my
Eight Sleep sleep tracker,

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:42.390
and I use a WHOOP sleep
tracker when I sleep.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:44.130
The combination of those two

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:46.860
taps into different
aspects of sleep tracking,

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:48.960
heart rate variability,
movement, et cetera.

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:50.460
In a future episode, we'll talk about

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:52.470
what are the best devices
for monitoring sleep,

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:55.890
which have certain advantages
and others have disadvantages.

00:21:55.890 --> 00:21:58.470
But I certainly see it
in the objective data

00:21:58.470 --> 00:22:00.510
that is from the sleep tracking data.

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:02.730
And I also observe subjectively

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:05.490
that taking 900 milligrams
of inositol prior to sleep,

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:07.560
again 30 to 60 minutes prior to sleep,

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:10.920
can greatly enhance the
ability to fall asleep

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:12.600
and for me to stay asleep
throughout the night.

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:13.860
I also noticed another effect,

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:15.540
which is that if I wake up
in the middle of the night

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:17.400
to use the restroom, which I often do,

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.040
I find it much easier to fall back asleep.

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:22.140
I don't tend to get into
kind of looping thought

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:23.970
and things of that sort in
the middle of the night.

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:26.790
And that may relate to
the fact that myo-inositol

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:29.370
has been studied in many cases

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:32.190
for its role in controlling anxiety

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:33.720
that is for reducing anxiety.

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:35.910
And that's one of the reasons, in fact,

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:38.400
one of many reasons why
people take myo-inositol

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:39.510
during the daytime.

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:40.920
There are studies of myo-inositol

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:44.730
for dealing with anxiety, for
sleep as we're discussing now,

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:46.470
and even for improving fertility

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:49.020
and things of that sort,
in particular in women.

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:51.060
So again, 900 milligrams of an inositol

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:52.320
can be a useful tool.

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:55.170
60 minutes of exercise,
as described previously,

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:58.530
can be a tool, and
certainly avoiding caffeine

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:01.410
and alcohol is an excellent tool

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:04.500
for enhancing the total
amount of slow-wave sleep.

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:06.840
Jack had a second part to his question,

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:10.050
he asked is having a high level
of rapid eye movement sleep

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:11.610
and a lower level of deep sleep

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:13.710
likely to cause any health issues?

00:23:13.710 --> 00:23:16.890
And to be honest, it's not really clear

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:19.350
that it would cause any
specific health issues,

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:21.480
although if we think about
what the specific roles

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:24.900
of slow-wave sleep versus
rapid eye movement sleep are,

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:26.730
again, rapid eye movement sleep

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:29.670
being laden with highly emotional dreams,

00:23:29.670 --> 00:23:32.280
It's thought to act as
a sort of trauma release

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:34.470
where people are paralyzed in body

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:35.940
and that's a healthy paralysis,

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:37.800
they're so-called sleep atonia,

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:40.530
but undergoing some pretty emotional stuff

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:42.060
within their dreams,

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:44.460
and there is a lack of
ability for the body

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:46.770
to secrete adrenaline during
rapid eye movement sleep,

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:47.970
that's thought to be important

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:51.030
for emotional repair, if you will,

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:52.440
during the night.

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:54.570
Certainly studies where
people have been deprived

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:56.070
of rapid eye movement sleep

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:58.890
have shown that their
ability to manage emotionally

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:00.480
during the daytime is reduced.

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:02.760
Certainly if you have reduced
rapid eye movement sleep

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:05.730
for multiple nights, that's
going to be the case.

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:07.770
The only caveat to that is
that there are certain forms

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:09.690
of treating depression that involve

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:12.600
specifically depriving people
of rapid eye movement sleep.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:15.360
That's a whole other
story that was covered

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:17.880
in the podcast episode
with Dr. Nolan Williams.

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:20.070
So if you want to learn more
as to why that's the case,

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:21.600
check out that episode.

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.880
But because slow-waves sleep,

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:25.860
which occurs again during
the early part of the night,

00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:28.080
is when growth hormones is released

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:31.560
and when there's this periodic washout

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:33.510
of debris within the brain,

00:24:33.510 --> 00:24:36.510
and to some extent within
certain tissues of the body,

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:38.640
you could imagine that
reduced low-wave sleep

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:42.210
would lead to reduced ability
to recover from exercise,

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:45.450
from injury, maybe even
relate to the immune system.

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:48.900
However, I am personally
not aware of any studies

00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:50.880
that specifically looked at the ratio

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:53.400
of slow-wave sleep to
rapid eye movement sleep,

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:57.330
and that focused in particular
on reduced slow-wave sleep

00:24:57.330 --> 00:24:58.260
for bodily repair.

00:24:58.260 --> 00:24:59.310
Those studies might be out there,

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:00.420
I was not able to find them.

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:01.290
If you are able to find them,

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:04.470
please put them in the comment section

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:06.600
and I would love to
check out those studies.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:09.870
So I don't think that one
needs to be overly concerned

00:25:09.870 --> 00:25:13.620
if you're not getting a
balance of slow-wave sleep

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:15.840
to rapid eye movement
sleep, that's perfect.

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:18.990
However, and as I mentioned earlier,

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:21.810
it would be good to
strive to try and balance

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:25.200
the amount of slow-wave sleep
and rapid eye movement sleep

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:26.760
across the entire night.

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:27.593
If you think about it,

00:25:27.593 --> 00:25:28.710
that will also tell you whether or not

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:30.870
you're getting sufficient total sleep.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:33.630
And I don't want to open up
another set of questions here

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:36.300
as I'm about to close out
an answer to this question,

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:37.980
but I do often get asked the question

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:39.870
whether or not four hours is enough,

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:42.750
or six hours is enough,
or eight hours is enough.

00:25:42.750 --> 00:25:44.670
Well, it's impossible to know exactly

00:25:44.670 --> 00:25:47.130
how much sleep each
individual person needs.

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:49.230
And of course, each individual person

00:25:49.230 --> 00:25:50.670
will need a different
amount of total sleep

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:52.110
depending on the amount of stress

00:25:52.110 --> 00:25:54.810
and physical strain and
various other things

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:58.320
they're dealing with in
their daily waking life.

00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:00.810
However, it's generally the case

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:03.420
that kids and people that
are undergoing puberty

00:26:03.420 --> 00:26:04.620
and certainly in early infancy

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:06.180
need more sleep than adults do.

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:09.210
So as you age, you do
in fact need less sleep.

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:10.680
One good metric of whether

00:26:10.680 --> 00:26:12.030
or not you are getting enough sleep

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:13.080
is whether or not you're sleepy

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:14.220
during the daytime or not.

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:15.990
If you're falling asleep
a lot during the daytime,

00:26:15.990 --> 00:26:17.610
you're probably not getting
enough sleep at night.

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:18.900
That's sort of a duh,

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:21.900
but it's an important
duh to pay attention to.

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:23.550
And for those of us, like myself,

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:27.240
that need a 20 or 30 minute
nap in the afternoon, great.

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:28.110
That doesn't necessarily mean

00:26:28.110 --> 00:26:29.690
you're not getting enough sleep at night.

00:26:29.690 --> 00:26:32.848
So if you get sleepy enough to
want a nap in the afternoon,

00:26:32.848 --> 00:26:35.220
that's not an indication necessarily

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:37.740
that you are not getting
enough sleep at night.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:40.350
However, if you're only sleeping four

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:41.940
or five hours per night,

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:44.100
it's very hard to imagine
that you're getting

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:47.550
a balance of slow-wave sleep
and rapid eye movement sleep.

00:26:47.550 --> 00:26:50.820
Once you get into the range
of sleeping six to eight hours

00:26:50.820 --> 00:26:53.730
and certainly 8 to 10 hours per night,

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:55.890
the probability that
you're getting a balance

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:57.840
of slow-wave sleep and
rapid eye movement sleep

00:26:57.840 --> 00:26:59.550
greatly increases.

00:26:59.550 --> 00:27:02.040
So if I were to throw out a number,

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:03.720
I would say for most people,

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:06.330
that is for 95% of people out there,

00:27:06.330 --> 00:27:10.170
getting at least six hours
of sleep per night, at least,

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:12.060
and ideally more like seven or eight

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:13.080
is going to be the goal.

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:15.390
However, I've noticed,
for instance, for myself,

00:27:15.390 --> 00:27:18.360
unless I'm exercising extremely intensely

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:20.400
or I'm going through a lot of emotional

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:22.170
or physical stress in my daily life,

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:24.420
getting six and a half to
seven hours of sleep per night

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:26.100
allows me to feel really good

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:27.810
and refreshed throughout the day.

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:29.850
And that's especially the case if I get

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:32.640
that 20 or 30 minute nap in the afternoon

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:35.400
or use NSDR, Non-Sleep Deep Rest.

00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:37.260
So I hope that answers
your question, Jack,

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:38.880
and there are, of course,

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:41.610
I'm extending the answer to all others

00:27:41.610 --> 00:27:43.290
who upvoted that question

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:45.030
or who might be interested in improving

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:46.440
their slow-wave sleep.

00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:47.790
There are some dos, there are some don'ts

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:49.335
that I just described.

00:27:49.335 --> 00:27:53.370
I suggest trying various things one by one

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:55.020
to see what works best for you

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:56.490
so you don't necessarily need to leap

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:58.980
to doing the 60 minutes of exercise

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:00.390
plus the inositol, et cetera.

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:03.750
However, I do encourage
everyone to avoid alcohol

00:28:03.750 --> 00:28:06.300
and caffeine within the 8
to 12 hours prior to sleep.

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:08.490
And again, if you're not
using either of those,

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:09.450
that would be even better,

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:11.760
at least in terms of sleep architecture.

